---
import Section from './Section.astro';
import { GITHUB_REPO_URL, SCOPE_LAW, SCOPE_TEMPLATES } from '../data/content';
---
<Section id="about" label="This isn't an attack on Takealot">
  <div class="tb-about__grid two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 class="display h2 tb-about__head">
        About this site, <em class="tb-about__italic">honestly and upfront.</em>
      </h2>
      <div class="row tb-about__chips">
        <span class="chip accent">Non-commercial</span>
        <span class="chip">Independent</span>
        <span class="chip">CC BY 4.0</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose tb-about__prose">
      <p><strong>Most of the time, Takealot gets it right.</strong> Most returns go smoothly. Most refunds process quickly. This site exists for the minority of cases where things don't, and specifically for the clauses in their returns policy that, in our view, stray beyond what the Consumer Protection Act actually permits.</p>
      <p>We read the law. We read their policy. Where the two disagree, we explain why, in plain English, with the statute numbers you can cite back to them.</p>
      <p><strong>Why Takealot?</strong> It's South Africa's <a href="/citations/press/knowler-cooling-off-exclusions-timeslive-2023-11-24" class="link-u">biggest online retailer</a>, so its returns policy reaches more shoppers than anyone else's. That's why we started here. But the rights on this site come from South African law, not from Takealot. {SCOPE_LAW} So the same rights apply when you buy from Amazon, Makro or the small online shop you found on Instagram. {SCOPE_TEMPLATES}</p>

      <h3 class="tb-about__subhead">The short version</h3>
      <p>TakealotBack.com is an independent, non-commercial consumer education site. We explain South African consumer law as it applies to online retail disputes, and we specifically publish commentary and criticism on Takealot's published returns policy.</p>
      <p>We use the Takealot name to say whose policy we're writing about. Our criticism and commentary are expression, protected by section 16 of the Constitution (freedom of expression).</p>

      <h3 class="tb-about__subhead">What we are not</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>We are not Takealot's customer support. If you need to log a return, that happens at <a href="https://takealot.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">takealot.com</a>.</li>
        <li>We are not attorneys. Nothing on this site is legal advice. <a href="/limits">Here's where the CPA's protection stops</a>.</li>
        <li>We are not a complaints directory. We do not publish user submissions.</li>
        <li>We are not anti-Takealot.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="tb-about__subhead">Legal grounding</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/citations/statutes/constitution-s16" class="link-u">Section 16 of the Constitution</a> (freedom of expression).</li>
        <li><a href="/citations/statutes/tma-1993" class="link-u">Section 34 of the Trade Marks Act 194 of 1993</a> read with <em><a href="/citations/cases/laugh-it-off-zacc-7-2005" class="link-u">Laugh It Off Promotions CC v SAB International</a></em> [2005] ZACC 7: a trade-mark proprietor who invokes the anti-dilution provision, s 34(1)(c), against expressive use must prove a likelihood of substantial economic detriment to the mark, and that claim is weighed against the constitutional right to expression.</li>
        <li>Common law fair comment.</li>
        <li>UDRP paragraph 4(c)(iii): legitimate non-commercial fair use.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="tb-about__subhead">Information only: no correspondence</h3>
      <p>This site has no contact email, no contact form, no comments. That is intentional. The site is a one-way reference resource. If you find something factually wrong, <a href={GITHUB_REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the site's GitHub repository is public: open an issue</a>.</p>

      <h3 class="tb-about__subhead" id="privacy">Privacy</h3>
      <p>Milk Moon Studio runs this site. We count visits so we can see which pages people use. That's all we use the data for. No ads, no accounts, no forms, and we don't sell anything to anyone.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> counts page views. It sets no cookies.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Analytics 4</strong>, run through Cloudflare Zaraz, sets cookies (<code>cfz_google-analytics_v4</code>, <code>cfzs_google-analytics_v4</code>, <code>cf_zaraz_client</code>) and records the pages you view, a random client ID, your approximate location, and your device and browser type.</li>
        <li>Cloudflare, which hosts the site, may also set a security cookie (<code>cf_clearance</code>) to tell people from bots.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Cloudflare and Google receive this data, and both may process it outside South Africa. Google may also use it for its own purposes, as it explains in <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How Google uses information from sites that use its services</a>. We never get your name or email, and you don't have to give us anything to use the site.</p>
      <p>To opt out, block cookies for takealotback.com in your browser settings, or use a content blocker that blocks <code>/cdn-cgi/zaraz/</code>. Google's <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">opt-out add-on</a> works on Google's own script, which this site doesn't load (Zaraz sends the data from Cloudflare's servers), so a content blocker is the surer route. Blocking the analytics cookies doesn't change how the site works.</p>
      <p>Questions about your data, or a request under POPIA: <a href={GITHUB_REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open an issue on the GitHub repository</a> (issues are public, so leave out personal details). You can also complain to the <a href="/citations/regulators/info-regulator-2026-04-24" class="link-u">Information Regulator</a>.</p>

      <h3 class="tb-about__subhead">Sources you can check</h3>
      <p>Every statute, judgment, regulator detail, advisory note, and Takealot policy clause the site relies on is mirrored at <a href="/citations" class="link-u">/citations</a>, dated and attributed, with primary URLs and Wayback Machine archival links. Each clause expansion ends with a "Sources for this clause" line linking to the specific citations behind that clause.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .tb-about__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
    gap: clamp(var(--space-6), 5vw, 80px);
  }
  .tb-about__head { max-width: 14ch; }
  /* italic emphasis: see global `.display em` rule in base.css */
  .tb-about__chips { margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .tb-about__prose { max-width: 64ch; }
  /* Section subheads inside the About prose. Promoted from h4 → h3 to
   * keep heading levels gap-free for a11y; styling is restrained so they
   * don't fight the section h2 visually. */
  .tb-about__subhead {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 28px 0 var(--space-2);
  }
</style>
